.Op Ar command
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
renders one static website from the documentation that a Perl project
already keeps:
.Xr mdoc 7
manuals, POD sidecars, and Markdown.
It runs
.Xr mandoc 1 ,
.Xr lowdown 1
and
.Xr pod2man 1 ,
and wraps each result in one shared page layout.
The output is plain HTML and one stylesheet.
There is no templating language and no JavaScript.
.Pp
A project is a directory that holds a
.Pa .fuguwebrc
file, the site description.
Without
.Fl Fl project ,
.Nm
searches up from the current directory for that file.
The directory that holds it is the project root.
Every relative path in the description, and a relative
.Fl Fl out ,
resolves against the project root.
.Pp
The build reads nothing from the network, writes nothing outside the
output directory, and gives the same bytes for the same checkout.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
Print the usage message and exit.
.It Fl Fl project Ar dir
Use
.Ar dir
as the project root, and do not search for one.
.It Fl q , Fl Fl quiet
Do not print the informational messages, which carry the word
.Li INFO .
Errors, warnings and the problems that
.Cm check
finds still go to standard error.
The standard output and the exit status do not change.
.El
.Pp
The commands are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm init Op Ar dir
Write a starter
.Pa .fuguwebrc
into
.Ar dir ,
which defaults to the current directory.
The starter names the
.Ic url ,
the
.Ic footer ,
one page and one navigation entry.
It builds once
.Pa web/index.body.html
and
.Pa web/footer.body.html
exist.
The command refuses a directory that already holds a description.
.It Cm build Op Fl Fl out Ar dir
Render the whole site into the output directory.
.Fl Fl out
overrides the
.Ic out_dir
setting.
.Pp
The command first checks that every renderer is installed, and names
the first one that is not.
It reads every manual and every Markdown page of each language, and a
source that is not UTF-8 fails the build.
It runs
.Xr mandoc 1
in lint mode over every mdoc source, and a warning fails the build.
It then writes the stylesheet, the assets and each key directory.
It writes each page into each language tree, and then the pages of the
top level: the top-level
.Pa 404.html
when the description holds that page, and the forward
.Pa index.html .
Next it writes one page for each manual into each tree.
A page of another language than the default holds the outline of its
page in the default tree, and takes the id of each heading of it.
See
.Sx LANGUAGES .
It reads each body fragment and each footer as UTF-8, and each output
of a renderer.
A file that is not UTF-8 fails the build, and the message names it.
Last, it removes every file of the site that the description no longer
names, so a second build over one directory gives the same tree as the
first.
See
.Sx THE PAGE LAYOUT
and
.Sx THE OUTPUT DIRECTORY .
.It Cm check Oo Fl v Oc Op Fl Fl out Ar dir
Check a built site.
The command prints one line for each problem and exits with status 5.
It prints nothing and exits with status 0 when the site is good.
.Pp
The checks are these:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Every page and asset of the site exists and is not empty.
.It
On every page but the forward
.Pa index.html ,
the check finds a title, the banner link and the whole navigation.
A tree page takes the words of its tree, and any other page those of
the default tree.
.It
The check resolves each link of the chrome from the path of its page,
and holds the target of the link and not its form.
An absolute URL stays as it stands.
.It
The
.Ql html
element of every tree page carries the tag of its tree as
.Ql lang ,
and
.Ql dir="rtl"
when the language of the tree is right to left.
.It
The head of a tree page names the page, the same page in each
language, and the forward page, below the
.Ic url .
The head of the forward names the bare
.Ic url
as its canonical link and as
.Ql x-default ,
and the entry page of each language.
The head of any other page names none.
.It
On a site of more than one language, the banner of a tree page and of
the top-level
.Pa 404.html ,
and the body of the forward, list each language, in the order of the
declaration, with its name as the text.
A link of a tree page goes to the same page in that language.
A link of a page of the top level goes to the entry page of that
language.
No other page lists one.
.It
The link of the language of the page and the navigation link of the
page are the current ones, and no other link is.
.It
The chrome holds no class, no
.Ql <hr>
and no text separator.
.It
A relative
.Ic nav
href names a page of each tree, as it resolves from the path of that
tree.
.It
The forward
.Pa index.html
is a page of the default language.
Its head holds the charset, a meta refresh to the entry page of the
default tree, the title and the links to each language, in that order,
and nothing else.
Its title is the site name.
On a site of one language, its body holds one link to the entry page,
with the site name as its text.
The body holds no other reference, and no navigation.
.It
The top-level
.Pa 404.html
is a page of the default language, with the title of
.Pa 404.html
of the default tree.
Its head holds the charset, the viewport, the
.Ql <base> ,
the title and the stylesheet link, in that order, and nothing else.
Its
.Ql <main>
holds one
.Ql <section>
for each language, in the order of the declaration, and nothing else.
Each section holds the body of
.Pa 404.html
of the tree of its language, with no id, and with each relative link
from the site root.
A section whose text comes from another language carries the marks of
that language.
.It
Each heading of a tree page of another language than the default takes
the id of the heading at the same position of the page in the default
tree, and no id when that heading has none.
.It
Every local link, manual cross-reference, and fragment resolves to a
file of the site.
A reference with a query passes when its path names a file of the
site.
.It
No reference uses a
.Ql file\&:
URL, or reads as a URL scheme.
.It
No reference starts with a solidus.
The one exception is the
.Ql <base>
of the top-level
.Pa 404.html ,
and that page must carry it.
Its href is the path of the
.Ic url ,
and each relative link of the page resolves from the site root.
.It
Every page of each language tree is reachable from the entry page of
that tree, except a page marked
.Ic unlinked .
The walk stays inside the tree.
The pages of the top level and the key page take no such walk.
.It
The output holds the site and nothing else, at any depth.
.El
.Pp
A description with a
.Ic keys
block adds the checks of
.Sx THE KEY DIRECTORY .
.Pp
.Fl v ,
.Fl Fl verbose
also prints every external link.
The command never fetches one.
.It Cm clean Op Fl Fl out Ar dir
Remove the output directory.
The command deletes a tree without asking, so it removes a built site
and refuses anything else.
See
.Sx THE OUTPUT DIRECTORY .
.Pp
With
.Fl Fl out
the command runs without a loadable description.
It then knows the files of the top level and of the language trees
that the
.Ic language
blocks of the file declare.
It refuses a target that holds no stylesheet, because every build
writes one.
.It Xo Cm mint-key
.Fl Fl purpose Ar word
.Op Fl Fl type Ar type
.Fl Fl secret Ar path
.Op Fl Fl signer Ar path
.Op Fl Fl bind Ar stem Ns = Ns Ar path
.Op Fl Fl email Ar address
.Op Fl Fl expires Ar date
.Op Fl Fl dir Ar name
.Op Fl Fl bootstrap Fl Fl org Ar word Op Fl Fl url Ar prefix
.Xc
Mint the next key of a purpose.
The
.Ar type
defaults to
.Cm signify ,
and
.Cm openpgp
is the other type that this release mints.
An OpenPGP mint needs
.Fl Fl email ,
and it takes an optional
.Fl Fl expires .
A mint of another type takes neither option.
See
.Sx KEY ROTATION .
.It Xo Cm import-key
.Fl Fl purpose Ar word
.Op Fl Fl type Ar type
.Fl Fl file Ar path
.Fl Fl secret Ar path
.Op Fl Fl signer Ar path
.Op Fl Fl bind Ar stem Ns = Ns Ar path
.Op Fl Fl dir Ar name
.Op Fl Fl bootstrap Fl Fl org Ar word Op Fl Fl url Ar prefix
.Xc
Publish a key that another tool made, under the next name of the
purpose.
The command takes
.Fl Fl purpose ,
.Fl Fl signer
and
.Fl Fl bind
as a mint of that purpose does, with
.Fl Fl file
beside them.
The
.Ar type
defaults to
.Cm signify ,
and
.Cm openpgp
and
.Cm x509
are the other two.
An issuer makes an
.Cm x509
certificate, so this command is the one that publishes one.
It takes no
.Fl Fl email
and no
.Fl Fl expires ,
because it generates no key.
The block of an imported
.Cm openpgp
key therefore carries no
.Ic email ,
and the Web Key Directory serves no file for the address of its user
id.
The
.Pa KEYS
file of its own directory still holds that key.
The
.Ic Encryption
field of
.Pa security.txt
can still name it, when that directory is the one that names the
contact.
A directory with no
.Ic keys
block takes one from
.Fl Fl bootstrap ,
.Fl Fl dir ,
.Fl Fl org
and
.Fl Fl url ,
as a mint does.
.Fl Fl secret
names the private half of the key in
.Fl Fl file ,
and that file must stand.
The command writes no key file of its own, and that private half
signs the binding of the new key.
A mint refuses a
.Fl Fl secret
that stands already, because a mint writes one.
See
.Sx KEY ROTATION .
.It Xo Cm promote-key
.Fl Fl purpose Ar word
.Fl Fl retiring Ar path
.Op Fl Fl secret Ar path
.Op Fl Fl signer Ar path
.Op Fl Fl bind Ar stem Ns = Ns Ar path
.Op Fl Fl dir Ar name
.Xc
Make the
.Cm next
key of a purpose current, and retire the key that was current.
See
.Sx KEY ROTATION .
.It Cm status
Print one line for each part of a translation that does not follow its
source, and exit with status 1.
Print nothing and exit with status 0 when each part follows its source.
See
.Sx TRANSLATION STATUS .
.It Cm translated Ar file ...
Record that each
.Ar file
follows its default source now.
Each
.Ar file
is a path from the project root.
See
.Sx TRANSLATION STATUS .
.It Cm help Op Ar command
Print the usage of the tool, or of one command.
.El
.Sh THE SITE DESCRIPTION
The description is
.Pa .fuguwebrc
at the project root.
It is UTF-8, and a description that is not fails the load.
A setting is one
.Ar name No = Ar value
line.
A block opens with its type, a name, and a brace, and closes with a
brace on a line of its own.
A name that holds whitespace takes double quotes.
A block with a language tag in front of its name is a translation, and
.Sx LANGUAGES
states its rules.
A
.Ql #
starts a comment, and no value can hold one.
The description holds the words of the default language.
.Pp
The settings are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "module_root" -offset indent
.It Ic site
The site name of each language whose block names none.
It ends the title of each page of such a language, and it is the text
of the banner link.
This setting is required.
.It Ic url
The absolute URL of the site root.
It starts with
.Ql https://
or
.Ql http:// ,
and it ends in a solidus.
This setting is required.
.It Ic footer
The footer fragment of each language whose block names none, below the
source directory.
A description with no
.Ic footer
gives a site with no footer.
.It Ic out_dir
The output directory.
The default is
.Pa web/build .
.It Ic source_dir
The source directory.
The default is
.Pa web .
It must not name the project root, such as
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa ./ .
The project root holds the record of
.Sx TRANSLATION STATUS ,
and the build publishes each plain file of the source directory.
.It Ic entry
The front page of each language tree, and the target of the banner
link.
The default is
.Pa index.html .
.It Ic module_root
The prefix that a module name drops.
The default is
.Pa lib .
.It Ic man_url
The manual host for a cross-reference to a manual outside the site.
The default is
.Lk https://man.openbsd.org/ .
.It Ic stylesheet
The path of the base stylesheet.
By default the build searches for the sheet that ships with the tool.
.El
.Pp
A path setting must not step out of the project.
.Pp
The block types are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic language Ar tag
One language of the site.
See
.Sx LANGUAGES .
.It Ic nav Qq Ar href
One navigation entry, in file order.
Every page carries it, except the forward
.Pa index.html
at the top level.
A relative
.Ar href
names a page of each tree, such as
.Pa manuals.html ,
and an absolute URL stays as it stands.
The block must set a
.Ic label .
.It Ic page Qq Ar file
One page of each language tree, named by its file in the tree.
The name must be one path segment.
The block sets a
.Ic title ,
which defaults to the file name, and exactly one source:
.Bl -tag -width "markdown" -offset indent
.It Ic body
An HTML body fragment in the source directory.
.It Ic markdown
A Markdown file, relative to the project root.
.Xr lowdown 1
renders it, and the HTML that it holds stays as written.
.It Ic index No = Cm yes
The generated manual index.
The page can also set a
.Ic body ,
which opens the index below its heading.
.El
.Pp
.Ic unlinked No = Cm yes
marks a page that no other page links to, such as
.Pa 404.html ,
and the reachability check skips it.
.It Ic manuals Ar name
One group of
.Xr mdoc 7
manuals.
The name is one word of lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, and it is
the fragment identifier of the group on the index page.
Two groups must not share a name.
The block must set
.Ic heading ,
the text of the heading of the group, and
.Ic dir ,
the directory of the sources.
An optional
.Ic namespace
prefixes each manual name, so
.Pa man/fugu/Daemon.3p
with the namespace
.Ql Fugu:\&:
becomes
.Ql Fugu::Daemon(3p) .
.It Ic modules Ar name
One group of POD sidecars.
The name follows the rules of a
.Ic manuals
name.
The block must set
.Ic heading
and
.Ic dir ,
and
.Ic dir
must lie below
.Ic module_root .
.It Ic keys Qq Ar name
One published key directory.
A description can hold several, and two blocks must not name one
directory.
See
.Sx THE KEY DIRECTORY .
.El
.Pp
The load fails for a block type or a setting that this manual does not
document, and the message names the block and the word.
A setting of an older description fails the same way.
The load fails when a block names no source or two sources, when two
blocks name one page, or when a group directory does not exist.
It fails when two manuals, or a manual and a
.Ic page
block, become one page.
Each test of two names that become one page folds the two, as
.Sx THE OUTPUT DIRECTORY
states.
It fails when the path of a source from the project root holds ASCII
white space or a parenthesis.
A source is a page source, a manual or a footer fragment, of any
language.
A symlink at the top level of the source directory fails the load.
A symlink below the source directory on the path of a fragment that the
build reads fails it too.
A group block whose name is no group name fails the load.
Every message names the file and the block at fault.
.Sh LANGUAGES
A
.Ic language
block declares one language of the site.
The block name is the tag of the language, and the order of the blocks
is the order of the languages.
The first language is the default.
A description with no
.Ic language
block has the one language
.Ql en .
.Pp
A tag is a BCP 47 tag, in its canonical case: a language subtag of two
or three lowercase letters, then an optional script subtag such as
.Ql Hans ,
then an optional region subtag such as
.Ql BR
or
.Ql 419 .
.Ql en ,
.Ql zh-Hans
and
.Ql pt-BR
are tags.
.Pp
The settings of a
.Ic language
block are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "footer" -offset indent
.It Ic name
The name of the language in that language, such as
.Ql English .
This setting is required.
.It Ic dir
The direction of the language:
.Cm ltr
or
.Cm rtl .
The default is
.Cm ltr .
.It Ic site
The site name in this language.
The default is the
.Ic site
of the description.
.It Ic footer
The footer fragment of this language, below the source directory.
The default is the
.Ic footer
of the description.
.El
.Pp
A translation is a block with the tag of a declared language in front
of its name.
The tag must not be the tag of the default language.
The block translates the block of one argument of the same type and
name:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
language en {
	name = English
}

language de {
	name   = Deutsch
	footer = de/footer.body.html
}

page "index.html" {
	title = Home
	body  = index.body.html
}

page de "index.html" {
	title = Start
	body  = de/index.body.html
}

manuals commands {
	heading = Commands
	dir     = man/fuguweb
}

manuals de commands {
	heading = Befehle
	dir     = man/de/fuguweb
}
.Ed
.Pp
The build writes one tree for each language, below the tag in
lowercase, and each tree holds the same pages.
The tree of
.Ql zh-Hans
is
.Pa zh-hans/ .
A
.Ic page ,
a
.Ic nav ,
a
.Ic manuals
and a
.Ic modules
block can take a translation:
.Bl -tag -width "manuals, modules" -offset indent
.It Ic page
.Ic title ,
.Ic body
and
.Ic markdown .
.It Ic nav
.Ic label .
.It Ic manuals , modules
.Ic heading
and
.Ic dir .
.El
.Pp
A page of a language takes each value of its translation, and the value
of the default block where the translation holds none.
The site name and the footer of a language follow the same rule.
Each value records the language that it comes from, and
.Sx THE PAGE LAYOUT
marks a value of another language than the page.
.Pp
The
.Ic dir
of a group translation mirrors the default
.Ic dir .
A manual of a language comes from the file of the same relative path in
that directory, and from the default source when the directory holds no
such file.
A
.Ic modules
translation mirrors the umbrella sidecar as well.
A manual keeps the page name of its default source in every language.
.Pp
A page of another language than the default must hold the outline of
its page in the default tree: the same count of headings, at the same
levels, in the same order.
Each heading then takes the id of the heading at the same position of
that page, or no id when that heading has none.
Each link of the page to one of its headings names the new id.
A section reference of a manual,
.Ql \&.Sx ,
finds its heading by its text, and each other link by its old id.
A link to a heading that takes no id stays as it stands.
A link to a fragment of a page then works in each language.
A page whose outline differs fails the build, and the message names the
page, the language and the position of the first heading that differs.
.Pp
The load fails when a tag has another shape, or when two blocks declare
one tag.
It fails when a
.Ic language
block has no
.Ic name ,
or a
.Ic dir
of another word.
It fails when a translation names a tag that no
.Ic language
block declares, or the tag of the default language.
It fails when a translation names no default block of its type, when it
comes twice for one name and one language, or when it sets a setting
outside its list.
It fails when a manual source of a translation
.Ic dir
has no source of the same path in the default
.Ic dir .
A
.Ic language ,
a
.Ic keys
and a
.Ic key
block take no translation.
.Sh THE PAGE LAYOUT
Every source becomes one HTML body fragment, and the build wraps each
fragment in one fixed layout: the head, a banner, the navigation, the
fragment, and a footer.
A project decides the site name, the languages, the navigation, and
the footer prose.
The layout carries no class, no
.Ql <hr>
and no text separator: the stylesheet draws each rule and each
separator.
.Pp
Every page of the description sits in the tree of each language, below
.Pa <tree>/
of the output, where the tree is the tag of the language in lowercase.
Each file of the site has one path below the site root, and each link
of the layout goes from the directory of its page to that path.
A page of
.Pa en/
links the entry page as
.Pa index.html ,
and the key page links it as
.Pa ../en/index.html .
The
.Ql html
element of a page carries the tag of its language as
.Ql lang ,
and
.Ql dir="rtl"
when that language is right to left.
.Pp
The head of a tree page holds the charset, the viewport, the title,
the links to the page in each language, and the stylesheet link.
The links are one
.Ql rel="canonical"
link to the page itself, one
.Ql rel="alternate"
link for each language with its tag as
.Ql hreflang ,
and one alternate link with
.Ql hreflang="x-default"
to the forward page.
Each href is the
.Ic url ,
then the path of the page, such as
.Lk https://example.org/en/index.html .
The forward page is the bare
.Ic url .
.Pp
The banner links the site name to the entry page of the tree.
On a site of more than one language, the list of the languages follows
the link.
Each item links the same page in that language, with the
.Ic name
of the language as its text, and its tag as
.Ql lang
and
.Ql hreflang .
The navigation is one list of the
.Ic nav
blocks, in file order.
The link of the language of the page and the navigation link of the
page carry
.Ql aria-current="page" .
.Pp
A part of a page whose text comes from another language than the page
carries the tag of that language as
.Ql lang .
It carries
.Ql dir
too when the two directions differ.
The part is the site link of the banner, the link of a navigation
label, the
.Ql main
element of a body, the
.Ql footer ,
or a
.Ql section
of the top-level
.Pa 404.html .
The index page marks each part on its own element instead: the title,
the
.Ic body ,
each group heading and each manual description.
A page of
.Pa ar/
whose body comes from the source of
.Ql en
therefore carries
.Ql <main lang="en" dir="ltr"> .
.Pp
The top level holds two pages for the host.
Each one is a page of the default language.
The forward
.Pa index.html
leads to the entry page of the default tree with a meta refresh.
Its title is the site name of the default language alone.
Its head names the bare
.Ic url
as its canonical link and as
.Ql x-default ,
and the entry page of each language as an alternate link.
On a site of more than one language, its body lists each language, and
each item links the entry page of that language.
On a site of one language, its body holds one link to the entry page.
The forward carries no banner, no navigation and no footer, and it
loads no stylesheet.
.Pp
When the description holds the page
.Pa 404.html ,
the top level holds one more
.Pa 404.html ,
with a
.Ql <base>
whose href is the path of the
.Ic url ,
such as
.Ql <base href="/"> .
A host serves that page at each path that no file answers, and each
relative link of the page resolves from the site root.
Its banner and its navigation link the tree of the default language,
as
.Pa en/index.html .
On a site of more than one language, the list of the languages follows
the site link, and each item links the entry page of that language.
Its head names no page in another language.
Its
.Ql <main>
holds the body of
.Pa 404.html
of each language, each one in its own
.Ql <section> ,
in the order of the declaration.
The build removes each id of a body, so the page holds no id twice.
It resolves each relative link of a body against
.Pa 404.html
of its tree.
In the body of
.Ql zh-Hans ,
.Pa index.html
becomes
.Pa zh-hans/index.html ,
and
.Ql #x
becomes
.Ql zh-hans/404.html#x .
.Pp
On a tree page and on the top-level
.Pa 404.html ,
the title is the title of the page, an em dash, and the site name.
.Pp
The fragment that the
.Ic footer
setting names becomes the footer prose of each tree page.
A language takes the footer of its
.Ic language
block, and the one of the description when its block names none.
The key page and the pages of the top level carry no footer.
A project without one gets no footer element.
.Pp
The base stylesheet ships with the tool as
.Pa share/fuguweb/style.css .
The build copies it to
.Pa style.css
at the top level of the output, outside every tree.
Each page but the forward links it from its own directory: a tree
page and the key page as
.Pa ../style.css ,
and the top-level
.Pa 404.html
as
.Pa style.css .
The
.Ic stylesheet
setting names another sheet.
A stylesheet that is not found fails the build.
.Sh MANUALS
A
.Ic manuals
group reads its directory for the plain files
.Pa *.1 ,
.Pa *.3p ,
.Pa *.5 ,
.Pa *.7
and
.Pa *.8 .
It sorts them by section, in the order 1, 3p, 5, 7, 8, and then by
file name.
The build renders each source with
.Xr mandoc 1
and names the page
.Ql <name>.<section>.html .
A cross-reference to a manual of the site becomes a local link.
Any other cross-reference links to the
.Ic man_url
host.
.Pp
The build removes the head and the foot that
.Xr mandoc 1
writes around a manual, so each word of the page comes from the
source.
The base
.Xr mandoc 1
of
.Ox
also writes a
.Ql <main>
element around the text, and the build replaces it with a
.Ql <div> ,
so a page holds one
.Ql <main> .
The page opens with one
.Ql <h1>
that holds the name and the section, such as
.Ql fuguweb(1) .
Each section heading is an
.Ql <h2>
and each subsection heading an
.Ql <h3> ,
and a heading holds no link to itself.
The page holds each character outside ASCII as the character, and not
as a character reference.
.Pp
A
.Ic modules
group finds every
.Pa .pod
file below its directory, and the sidecar that names the directory
itself:
.Pa lib/App/FuguWeb.pod
is the umbrella of
.Pa lib/App/FuguWeb/ .
The manual name is the path below
.Ic module_root
with
.Ql :\&:
between the segments, and the section is
.Ql 3p .
The build renders each sidecar with
.Xr pod2man 1
and then
.Xr mandoc 1 ,
so a module page carries the same layout as a hand-written manual.
A
.Ql L<>
reference renders as text, not as a link.
.Pp
The index page is the
.Ic page
block with
.Ic index No = Cm yes .
Its heading is the title of the page, and its
.Ic body ,
when it sets one, follows the heading.
The index writes no prose of its own.
Each group then adds one heading, with the group name as its fragment
identifier and the
.Ic heading
of the language as its text, and one list of its manuals.
Each entry links the page and shows the one-line description, from the
first
.Ic \&Nd
macro of an mdoc source or the
.Sy NAME
section of a sidecar.
A group with no manual adds nothing.
.Sh THE SOURCE DIRECTORY
The source directory holds the body fragments, the footer, and the
assets.
Every plain file directly in it that the build does not render is an
asset, and the build copies it as it stands.
A
.Pa *.body.html
fragment, a
.Pa *.md
file, and a dot file are not assets.
.Pa robots.txt
and
.Pa CNAME
therefore need no entry in the description, and notes for the
maintainers are not published.
.Pp
The build copies each asset to the top level of the output, outside
every language tree.
A tree page sits one directory below the top level.
A body fragment, a Markdown page and the footer of a tree page
therefore link an asset as
.Pa ../<asset> .
.Pp
The body of
.Pa 404.html
takes the same rule.
The build resolves each relative link of it against its own tree, also
in the top-level
.Pa 404.html .
.Pp
The build rejects a symlink at the top level of the source directory.
It also rejects a symlink on the path of each fragment that it reads.
The rule holds for the file, and for each directory between the source
directory and the file.
These fragments are the body fragment of each page in each language,
and the footer of each language.
An asset or a fragment would publish the bytes of the target, from
anywhere on the machine.
.Sh THE OUTPUT DIRECTORY
The top level of the output holds the two pages for the host, the
stylesheet and the assets.
Each language tree is one flat directory of pages below it.
The key directory and the well-known tree are the other parts below the
top level.
.Pp
The build writes plain files only.
It refuses a symlink on any path that it writes, before it writes one
file.
The test folds each name, so it also refuses a link at
.Pa INDEX.HTML .
It owns a plain file at the top level, and a plain file directly in the
tree of a declared language.
It also owns a file that the site names, and a file that has a shape of
the key directory.
The build keeps every other entry, reports it, and never removes it.
The tree of a language that the description dropped is therefore kept
and reported.
.Cm clean
removes what the build owns, and a directory of the site, the tree of a
declared language, or a directory of the key tree.
It refuses every other entry, and names it, so it never removes a file
that the build keeps.
.Pp
Both
.Cm build
and
.Cm clean
refuse a target that no build may own: the root of the filesystem, the
home directory, the project root, and any directory that holds the
project.
They refuse the source directory and every directory below it.
The output directory of the description is the one exception, because
the default output directory sits inside the default source directory.
.Pp
Both commands also refuse a target that holds a source.
The build removes each file of the top level that the site does not
name, and
.Cm clean
removes the whole target, so either one would remove the source.
A source is a body, a footer, a Markdown page, a manual or a POD
sidecar of each language, or the
.Ic stylesheet .
The source directory, each key directory and the
.Ic dir
of each group in each language are sources too.
Both commands also refuse a target inside a key directory or inside the
.Ic dir
of a group.
The exception does not cover these refusals, and the message names the
target and the source.
A description that does not load names its source directory and its
key directories alone.
.Pp
Each test that refuses a name, a target or a link because it meets
another name compares the folds of the two: the NFD form of Unicode,
then the full case fold.
A file system without case, such as APFS, keeps two names of one fold
as one entry.
The symlink test, each refusal of the target, and each test of a page
name or a key directory name against another name fold.
Each other test compares the names as they stand, so a fold can only
make a test refuse more.
The exception of the output directory of the description is such a
test.
So is each test of the prune of the build and of
.Cm clean
that decides which entry the build owns.
The fold reads the Unicode version of
.Xr perl 1 .
A file system of a later version can fold a character that
.Xr perl 1
does not know, and a test then misses that pair.
.Pp
The build stages every manual source in
.Pa .man/<tree>
inside the output directory, one directory for each language, so that
.Xr mandoc 1
resolves a cross-reference against the manuals of the site.
The build removes the staging directory when it ends.
It refuses a staging directory of another shape, and so does
.Cm clean .
.Sh THE KEY DIRECTORY
An organization publishes its public keys under one prefix, so a
consumer can fetch a key and verify a release with it.
A
.Ic keys
block describes that directory, and the build publishes it.
A description can hold several such blocks, and each one names one
directory.
Two blocks must not name one directory.
.Pp
Each directory holds its own root of trust, and a binding never
crosses a directory.
A publisher that keeps two trust domains therefore keeps two
directories in one site.
The site-level files stay single: one
.Pa security.txt ,
and one Web Key Directory that gathers every address.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
keys "keys" {
	org     = fugubsd
	contact = mailto:security@fugubsd.org
	expires = 2027-09-07T00:00:00Z
	url     = https://www.fugubsd.org/keys
}

key "fugubsd-1-release" {
	status = current
	since  = 2026-09-07
}
.Ed
.Pp
The block name is the directory, one path segment below the source
directory.
The tree of a declared language takes its path in the output, so a name
of that path is refused.
Each test of the name against another name of the output folds the
two, as
.Sx THE OUTPUT DIRECTORY
states.
The settings are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "contact" -offset indent
.It Ic org
The organization word, the first field of every key name.
This setting is required.
Two blocks must not name one word.
A key name holds no directory name, and the serial of a purpose counts
inside one directory, so a mint of the second block would write a name
that the first block publishes.
.It Ic contact
The
.Ql Contact
field of
.Pa security.txt .
.It Ic expires
The
.Ql Expires
field of
.Pa security.txt ,
an RFC 3339 timestamp.
.Ic contact
and
.Ic expires
stand or fall together.
A site publishes one
.Pa security.txt ,
so one block alone names the two.
.It Ic url
The published prefix of the directory, an absolute URL.
.El
.Pp
Each
.Ic key
block names the stem of one key file in the directory.
The file extension gives the type:
.Pa .pub
is a
.Xr signify 1
key,
.Pa .asc
is an armored OpenPGP key, and
.Pa .pem
is one X.509 certificate.
The block sets a
.Ic status
of
.Cm current ,
.Cm next
or
.Cm retired ,
and optional
.Ic since
and
.Ic until
dates.
An OpenPGP key can set an
.Ic email
and a
.Ic fingerprint
of 40 hexadecimal characters.
A certificate can set a
.Ic fingerprint
of 64 hexadecimal characters, which is the SHA-256 of its DER form.
.Pp
One signify key of the directory is the root of trust, and its purpose
word is
.Cm root .
The current root key signs the manifest, and every other key of the
directory is a subordinate key.
A binding is a detached signature by one key over the public key file
of another key, and its name is
.Pa <target file>.<signer stem>.<ext> .
The extension is
.Pa sig
for a
.Xr signify 1
signer,
.Pa asc
for an OpenPGP signer, and
.Pa p7s
for an X.509 signer.
A binding needs no
.Ic key
block: its name holds the target, the signer and the type.
.Pp
The directory must hold a
.Pa SHA256
manifest and its
.Pa SHA256.sig
signature.
The build copies each key file, each binding file and the manifest
pair byte for byte.
It signs nothing, and it verifies no
.Pa SHA256.sig .
For the example above it generates these files:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width ".well-known/openpgpkey/hu/<hash>" -offset indent -compact
.It Pa keys/KEYS
Every OpenPGP key, when the set holds one.
.It Pa keys/index.html
The human page, with the serial, purpose, type, status, fingerprint,
dates and bindings of each key.
The row of a certificate names its subject and its validity dates
beside the fingerprint.
The page is a page of
.Ql en ,
because its own words are English.
Its title is
.Ql Keys
alone, and its head names no page in another language.
Its banner holds the site link alone, and its navigation is that of the
default language.
Each of those links steps out of the key directory into the tree of
the default language, as
.Pa ../en/index.html .
When the default language is not
.Ql en ,
each of those links carries the marks of the default language: its tag
as
.Ql lang ,
and
.Ql dir="rtl"
when that language is right to left.
The page carries no footer.
.It Pa .well-known/openpgpkey/hu/<hash>
The Web Key Directory file of each email address.
The file holds the keys of that address of every directory, in
publication order.
.It Pa .well-known/openpgpkey/policy
The Web Key Directory policy file.
.It Pa .well-known/security.txt
The RFC 9116 file, when a block sets a
.Ic contact .
The
.Ic Encryption
field names the current OpenPGP key of that block.
.El
.Pp
.Cm check
reads the source key directory, so the answer does not depend on a
build.
It checks that every key file has a block and every block has a file,
that every name matches the key name pattern or the binding name
pattern, that every purpose holds exactly one current key, that the
directory holds one current signify key of the purpose
.Cm root ,
that each binding verifies against the public key of its signer and
holds the retention rule, that the manifest names every key file and
every binding file with the digest that it has and nothing else, that
every declared fingerprint equals the computed one, and that the
signature has the shape of a
.Xr signify 1
signature.
.Pp
One report reads the whole site.
The block which names the contact and a
.Ic url
must hold a
.Cm current
OpenPGP key of its own, because the
.Ic Encryption
field names no key of another directory.
The check reports that block when another key directory holds such a
key.
A site that publishes no OpenPGP key at all writes no such field, and
that one is no problem.
.Pp
The retention rule holds each binding of the directory.
A binding whose signer is
.Cm current
or
.Cm next
must target the current root key.
A binding whose signer is
.Cm retired
must target a key of its own purpose with a higher serial.
A signify binding verifies with no command.
A binding of another type needs
.Xr gpg 1
or
.Xr openssl 1 ,
and an absent command is a problem.
.Pp
The check reads the validity of each
.Cm current
and
.Cm next
key that carries one.
It reads the expiry of an OpenPGP key with
.Xr gpg 1 ,
and the
.Ql notBefore
and
.Ql notAfter
of a certificate from the certificate itself.
An expiry that passed is a problem, and so is a certificate whose
.Ql notBefore
has not come.
An expiry within 30 days of a
.Cm current
key is a problem when its purpose holds no
.Cm next
key, because one rotation runs in two steps.
A signify key carries no date, and an OpenPGP key with no expiry
reaches no rule.
.Pp
A key verb fails for none of these reports, so
.Cm mint-key
and
.Cm import-key
each run on a directory that
.Cm check
rejects for a date.
An expired
.Cm current
key is still
.Cm current
when the verb reads its work back, so a step of that purpose could
never be made.
The 30-day report reads the whole directory, so it would fail a step
of another purpose, which writes no
.Cm next
key of the purpose that the report names.
.Pp
.Cm promote-key
fails when the key that retires is an OpenPGP key whose expiry
passed.
That key signs the chain binding, and
.Xr gpg 1
signs nothing with an expired key.
Promote the successor before the current key expires.
.Sh KEY ROTATION
Every key verb writes one key directory.
.Fl Fl dir
names it.
A description with one
.Ic keys
block needs no name, and one with several refuses a step without it.
.Pp
A consumer verifies a release against the copy of the public key that
it holds.
A release that a new key signs therefore fails in each consumer that
holds the old copy.
One rotation runs in two steps.
.Pp
One signify key of the directory is the root of trust, and its purpose
word is
.Cm root .
The current root key signs the manifest, and every other key binds to
it.
.Cm mint-key
and
.Cm import-key
refuse a key of another type for the
.Cm root
purpose, and the mint refuses before it generates one.
Every step takes the private half of the current root as
.Fl Fl signer ,
with two exceptions below.
.Pp
.Cm mint-key
generates the next key of the purpose, writes its public half into the
key directory, and writes its private half to
.Fl Fl secret
with no group and no other permissions.
The new key takes the status
.Cm next ,
or
.Cm current
when the purpose holds no current key.
.Fl Fl secret
must not name a file that exists, and must not name the signer.
A mint refuses a purpose that already holds a
.Cm next
key.
A mint of a subordinate purpose also writes the binding of the new key
over the public key file of the current root.
.Pp
.Cm promote-key
makes the
.Cm next
key of the purpose current, and retires the key that was current with
an
.Ic until
date.
.Fl Fl retiring
names the private half of the key that retires, and that key signs the
chain binding over the key which takes its place.
A consumer that trusts the old key therefore reaches the new one.
The promote removes the root binding of the retired key, and the chain
binding stays published.
A retired key keeps its file and its URL, so a release that it signed
still verifies.
.Ss The key types
.Xr signify 1
generates a
.Cm signify
key.
The public half is one comment line and one key body.
.Pp
.Xr gpg 1
generates an
.Cm openpgp
key: one Ed25519 primary key with one Curve25519 encryption subkey.
.Fl Fl email
names the address, which is the one user id of the key, and a
correspondent encrypts to the subkey.
Both halves are armored text.
The
.Ic key
block of the new key takes that
.Ic email
and the
.Ic fingerprint ,
which the command reads from the key that it generated.
.Pp
.Fl Fl expires
is a date of the form
.Ar YYYY-MM-DD ,
and the key expires at the start of that date, in UTC.
The date must be after the day of the run.
The day of the run and each earlier date fail the mint, and the reason
names the date that you gave.
A mint with no
.Fl Fl expires
makes a key with no expiry.
A key directory retires a key with an
.Ic until
date, so a rotation needs no expiry on the key itself.
.Pp
An issuer makes an
.Cm x509
certificate, such as a code signing certificate of Apple Developer ID,
so no verb mints one.
.Cm import-key
publishes it:
.Fl Fl file
names one PEM certificate, and
.Fl Fl secret
names its unencrypted PEM private key.
The verbs take no passphrase, so convert a PKCS#12 file with
.Ic openssl pkcs12
first.
The private key signs the binding of the new key, and
.Xr openssl 1
makes that detached CMS signature.
The
.Ic key
block of the new key takes the
.Ic fingerprint ,
which the command reads from the certificate that it published.
The renderer knows no issuer by name, and the root manifest vouches
for the certificate.
A renewal is a rotation: the new certificate enters as
.Cm next ,
and a promote retires the old one.
.Ss The first root mint
The first mint of the
.Cm root
purpose finds no root.
That key is current at once, it signs its own manifest, and it takes
no
.Fl Fl signer .
A directory that already holds keys takes one
.Fl Fl bind Ar stem Ns = Ns Ar path
for each current key and each next key of a subordinate purpose, and
the step writes the binding of every one of them again.
It refuses before it writes when one private half is absent.
.Pp
A directory with no
.Ic keys
block takes one from the command line:
.Fl Fl bootstrap
states the intent to make that directory,
.Fl Fl dir
names it, which defaults to
.Pa keys ,
.Fl Fl org
names the organization word, and
.Fl Fl url
names the published prefix.
A second key directory of a site arrives the same way.
.Pp
A step without
.Fl Fl bootstrap
refuses a
.Fl Fl dir
that no
.Ic keys
block names.
Each key directory holds a root of trust of its own, so a mistyped
name would otherwise publish a second root in silence.
.Cm mint-key
and
.Cm import-key
take the option, and
.Cm promote-key
makes no directory.
.Ss The root promote
A root promote signs the manifest with the key that it makes current,
so it takes
.Fl Fl secret
and no
.Fl Fl signer .
It takes one
.Fl Fl bind
for each key in force, as the first root mint does, and it removes
each binding of the retired root but the chain binding that names it.
.Pp
Each step verifies the new signature and each new binding against the
published public key of the signer before one byte reaches the key
directory, and a step that fails puts every file back.
On success the command prints one
.Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value
line for each fact: the file name, the stem, the serial, the status,
the retired name of a promote, and the digest and the URL of a new key
file.
.Pp
The command reaches no network and holds no token.
The caller stores the private key and commits the public one.
.Sh TRANSLATION STATUS
A translation follows its source, and a source changes.
The record
.Pa TRANSLATIONS
at the project root names the version of the default source that each
translated file follows.
The build and the check do not read the record.
.Pp
The record holds one line for each translated file, in the form of a
SHA256 manifest:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
SHA256 (web/zh-hans/index.body.html) = <digest>
.Ed
.Pp
The path names the translated file from the project root.
It holds no
.Pa \&./
step, no empty step and no final solidus, so a path setting of
.Pa ./web
or
.Pa web/
gives the path
.Pa web/zh-hans/index.body.html .
The digest is the SHA-256 of its default source at the time of the
translation.
The lines sort by path.
A record that is absent or empty holds no line.
.Pp
.Cm translated
writes the line of each
.Ar file ,
with the digest that the default source of that file has now.
It changes each
.Ar file
to the form of a path of the record, so
.Pa ./web/a.html
and
.Pa web/a.html
name one file.
It keeps each other line of the record.
It refuses a file that translates no source of the description, such as
a default source, and then it writes nothing.
.Pp
Two parts that name one translated file must name one default source,
because the record holds one digest for each file.
.Cm status
and
.Cm translated
refuse a description that does not obey this rule, and name the file
and the two sources.
.Pp
.Cm status
prints one line for each part of a language other than the default
that does not follow its source.
A line holds four fields, with one tab between two fields: the tag of
the language, the state, the kind of the part, and its name.
The lines follow the order of the languages, and then the order of the
description.
.Pp
The states are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "unrecorded" -offset indent
.It Cm fallback
The part comes from the default language.
.It Cm unrecorded
The language translates the file, and the record holds no line for it.
.It Cm stale
The line of the file names a digest other than the one that its
default source has now.
.El
.Pp
The kinds of part, and the name of each, are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "heading" -offset indent
.It Cm site
The site name.
Its name is the tag of the language.
.It Cm label
The label of a
.Ic nav
entry.
Its name is the href of the entry.
.It Cm title
The title of a page.
Its name is the name of the page.
.It Cm page
The source of a page.
Its name is the name of the page.
.It Cm heading
The heading of a group.
Its name is the name of the group.
.It Cm manual
The source of a manual.
Its name is the page of the manual.
.It Cm footer
The footer fragment.
Its name is the tag of the language.
.El
.Pp
The site name, a label, a title and a heading are values of the
description, and a value is
.Cm fallback
or follows its source.
A file part follows no source when the default language names none for
it, and it then takes no line.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "share/fuguweb/style.css" -compact
.It Pa .fuguwebrc
The site description, at the project root.
.It Pa TRANSLATIONS
The record of the translations, at the project root.
.It Pa share/fuguweb/style.css
The base stylesheet, which ships with the tool.
.It Pa web/build/.man
The staging directory of the build, in the default output directory.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with one of the following values:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent -compact
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
General error, or a part of a translation that does not follow its
source, from
.Cm status ,
or a file that translates no source, from
.Cm translated .
.It 2
Invalid arguments.
.It 3
Configuration error, or no
.Pa .fuguwebrc
found.
.Cm status
and
.Cm translated
also exit with this value when they cannot read the record or a source,
or when two parts name one translated file over two default sources.
.It 4
The build failed: a renderer failed, or a manual source is malformed.
.It 5
The check found a problem.
.It 6
A renderer, or a command of a key step such as
.Xr signify 1 ,
.Xr gpg 1
or
.Xr openssl 1 ,
is not installed.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Start a site, write its front page and its footer, build it, and check
it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguweb init
$ mkdir web
$ echo "<h1>Home</h1>" >web/index.body.html
$ echo "<p>ISC license.</p>" >web/footer.body.html
$ fuguweb build
$ fuguweb check
.Ed
.Pp
Build into another directory, from anywhere in the project:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguweb build --out /tmp/site
$ fuguweb check --out /tmp/site
.Ed
.Pp
Publish the root key and the first release key of an organization:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguweb mint-key --purpose root --bootstrap \e
	--secret ~/keys/root-1.sec --org fugubsd \e
	--url https://www.fugubsd.org/keys
$ fuguweb mint-key --purpose release \e
	--secret ~/keys/release-1.sec --signer ~/keys/root-1.sec
.Ed
.Pp
Publish an OpenPGP contact key that expires at the start of 2028:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguweb mint-key --purpose contact --type openpgp \e
	--email security@fugubsd.org --expires 2028-01-01 \e
	--secret ~/keys/contact-1.sec --signer ~/keys/root-1.sec
.Ed
.Pp
Publish a code signing certificate, whose private key stays with the
publisher:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguweb import-key --purpose sign --type x509 \e
	--file ~/keys/sign-1.pem --secret ~/keys/sign-1.key \e
	--signer ~/keys/root-1.sec
.Ed
.Pp
Rotate the release key:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguweb mint-key --purpose release \e
	--secret ~/keys/release-2.sec --signer ~/keys/root-1.sec
$ fuguweb promote-key --purpose release \e
	--signer ~/keys/root-1.sec --retiring ~/keys/release-1.sec
.Ed
.Pp
Rotate the root key.
The promote writes the binding of each key in force again, so it takes
one
.Fl Fl bind
for each of them:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguweb mint-key --purpose root \e
	--secret ~/keys/root-2.sec --signer ~/keys/root-1.sec
$ fuguweb promote-key --purpose root \e
	--secret ~/keys/root-2.sec --retiring ~/keys/root-1.sec \e
	--bind fugubsd-2-release=~/keys/release-2.sec \e
	--bind fugubsd-1-contact=~/keys/contact-1.sec \e
	--bind fugubsd-1-sign=~/keys/sign-1.key
.Ed
.Pp
Publish a second key directory, for the keys of one project:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguweb mint-key --purpose root --dir project --bootstrap \e
	--secret ~/keys/project-root-1.sec --org fuguproj \e
	--url https://www.fugubsd.org/project
$ fuguweb mint-key --purpose release --dir project \e
	--secret ~/keys/project-release-1.sec \e
	--signer ~/keys/project-root-1.sec
.Ed
.Pp
Find a translation that no longer follows its source, update it, and
record it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguweb status
zh-Hans	stale	page	index.html
$ vi web/zh-hans/index.body.html
$ fuguweb translated web/zh-hans/index.body.html
$ fuguweb status
.Ed
